# Stereo-inspired: a forgiving cube-only first level.
# Each line: type column row width height, measured in grid cells.
# Row 0 rests on the floor. No portals, music, or ship mechanics required.
# About one minute at the current normal speed. The last spike ends at 624;
# the finish flag follows twelve clear cells later, at column 636.

# 1. Opening: establish a rhythm with singles, then introduce doubles.
# The first obstacle leaves a short approach from the starting position.
spike 20 0 1 1
spike 34 0 1 1
spike 48 0 1 1
spike 49 0 1 1
spike 64 0 1 1
spike 65 0 1 1

# Broad, one-cell-high platforms introduce landing without a precise high jump.
block 78 0 6 1
block 89 0 8 1

# 2. A staircase with long treads: each upward step is only one cell.
spike 108 0 1 1
spike 122 0 1 1
spike 123 0 1 1
block 136 0 10 1
block 146 0 10 2
block 156 0 12 3
block 168 0 8 2
spike 190 0 1 1
spike 191 0 1 1

# 3. First ship substitute: broad terraces under a high overhead beam.
# Jump up the steps; walking down is safe. There are no spike-filled gaps.
block 210 0 12 1
block 222 0 12 2
block 234 0 16 3
block 250 0 12 2
block 262 0 12 1
block 218 7 54 1

# 4. Back to the floor: combine platform landings and spikes on top.
spike 286 0 1 1
spike 302 0 1 1
spike 303 0 1 1
block 318 0 12 1
# Seven clear cells from the platform's leading edge to the spike.
spike 325 1 1 1
block 346 0 10 1
block 356 0 12 2

# Separate the harder jumps with clear floor for recovery.
spike 384 0 1 1
spike 385 0 1 1
spike 400 0 1 1
spike 401 0 1 1
spike 416 0 1 1
spike 417 0 1 1

# 5. Second ship substitute: repeat the broad rise-and-fall terraces.
block 448 0 12 1
block 460 0 12 2
block 472 0 16 3
block 488 0 12 2
block 500 0 12 1
block 456 7 54 1

# 6. Final reprise: familiar singles and doubles, with room to reset timing.
spike 532 0 1 1
spike 546 0 1 1
spike 547 0 1 1
spike 562 0 1 1
spike 563 0 1 1
block 582 0 14 1
spike 590 1 1 1
spike 606 0 1 1
